I wrote the "unfamiliar reader" review of the Grace Reiter draft to `review-unfamiliar.json` in the run folder. It passes the pipeline's review schema, and the checksums and role are copied from the request. Nothing else in the repo was touched, and nothing was published.

**How it reads to someone who's never heard of her:** it works well. The opening, where she says success makes her so guilty she's thinking of becoming a Christian, needs no background. The roommate's "you hate compliments more than anyone I know" makes it clear this is a pattern, not a one-off joke. The ending sets up a real test: if she's a Three, a season of SNL applause will teach her to believe the praise; if she's a Six, it won't.

**Two major problems:**
- **The size of her success is never shown in the body.** The argument is about how she handles a big hit, but the parody's ~14 million views appear only in the FAQ. The Netflix show is never introduced, yet a later sentence mentions "TikTok to HBO, Netflix and SNL."
- **The Type 3 section and the FAQ overstate two pieces of evidence.** The body itself says her "my own hand brings down myself" line was half a joke about an embarrassing podcast story. I confirmed that in the transcript. The summary and FAQ present it as her predicting her own downfall after her biggest hit. They also put the "give it to God" idea in the present tense, though the body shows she dropped it by April 2026.

**Six minor problems:**
- About 20 named people appear, mostly with no sense of how they know her. Close witnesses (roommate, sister, producer) blur into one-off podcast hosts, which weakens the "people around her see it too" argument.
- "A third host" refers to Drew Afualo before she's been introduced.
- Her comedy itself is never shown, so readers can't judge which quotes are bits.
- "The shakes" section drifts off its stage-fright thread and ends on a generic "For a Six…" line. Earlier sections end the same way.
- The description calls the guilt her "real" overshare. The only time the body uses "real overshare," it means the sad stories she keeps back.
- The final scene never says what praise she was wincing at.

I marked five passages to keep as they are, including the opening quote and the ending. The similarity flag (the shared phrase "core emotion") is ordinary Enneagram wording, not copied writing. I kept both carried-over risk flags on: contested typing, and sensitive family and therapy material.

This isn't a full fact-check. I only checked three transcript passages and one press article. The body runs 3,604 words, slightly over the 3,500 target.
